-- EXPECT-ERROR: which is non-deterministic
-- Capabilities widening, Task 5 (F2 fix) -- `cap deterministic`
-- (`check_deterministic_module`, `typecheck.ml:6650`), the REJECT side: a
-- `cap deterministic` module whose body reads the wall clock via the REAL
-- builtin `unix_time_ms` (`builtin_cap_table` maps it to `IO.Clock`,
-- `typecheck.ml:1061`). Pre-fix, `deterministic_banned` spelled `now_ms`
-- (a name that DOES NOT EXIST) and MISSED the real `unix_time_ms` -- so this
-- program typechecked with exit 0 (UNSOUND). The F2 fix derives
-- `deterministic_banned` from `builtin_cap_table`, keeping only the builtins
-- mapped to a NONDETERMINISM cap (`IO.Clock`/`IO.Random`); `unix_time_ms` is
-- now banned. `unix_time_ms : Unit -> Int` so `unix_time_ms(())` with a `:
-- Int` signature is type-correct, and the ONLY rejection is
-- `check_deterministic_module`'s own: `` `ts` in `mod LeakyDet` (declared
-- `cap deterministic`) calls `unix_time_ms`, which is non-deterministic. ``
-- Note `cap deterministic` remains WEAKER than `cap pure`: a deterministic
-- module may still do ordinary IO (e.g. `file_read`) -- only clock/RNG are
-- banned. (A WARNING-level Check 1b body-scan diagnostic -- "requires
-- `Cap(IO.Clock)`" -- also fires alongside, per F1; it does not change the
-- exit code and is not the substring pinned here.) See
-- core-march-types.md §2.8.11.
mod LeakyDet do
  cap deterministic

  fn ts() : Int do
    unix_time_ms(())
  end
end
